  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 640 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nTonight: A Few Showers and Storms Possible\n\nWe reside near the crest of a longwave ridge with multiple weak\nperturbations rippling over the ridge axis and across the\nregion. These have been responsible for a few clusters of light\nrain showers for a few locales late this afternoon. A quasi-\nstationary surface baroclinic zone sharpens overnight with\nincreasing low to mid tropospheric theta-e advection along this\nsurface. While the explicit CAM reflectivity outputs are tame, \na look at forecast soundings shows a nearly uncapped 1500-1700 \nJ/kg of MUCAPE rooted at 800 mb should the aforementioned \ntheta-e advection come to fruition. Therefore, have expanded low\nPoPs across much of the region--focused mainly east of the \nMississippi River where this MUCAPE signal is more pronounced. \nThe wind fields within the effective inflow layer are fairly \nweak and hodographs compact, therefore general thunderstorms \nwould be the main threat. Given the warmer cloud depths in \nexcess of 10 kft, these cells could be efficient rainfall \nproducers and combined with the line parallel steering motion, \nthere is a non- zero risk for training cells and rainfall \namounts over 1\" later tonight.\n\nSunday - Sunday Night: Shower and Storm Potential\n\nThe main story for Sunday is the potential for another round of\nshowers and storms in the afternoon. 850hPa moisture transport \nand WAA noses into a warm front draped across the region, \ngenerally along I-90, which is expected to provide enough \nforcing for convective development along and south of this \nboundary. Instability looks to be on the lower side given the \nexpected elevated nature of the showers/storms with MLCAPE \nsitting around 500-1000 J/kg across our region, higher to the\nnorthwest across central Minnesota. The more favorable \nkinematic environment for strong storms is also expected to \nexist north and west of the area, which is in line with where \nthe SPC and AI/ML guidance highlights the greatest potential for\na stronger storm.\n\nEarly Next Week: Warmer, Periodic Shower and Storm Chances Continue\n\nTemperatures warm as we head into the beginning of the new week\nas persistent southerly low level winds continue to usher \nwarmer air into the Upper Midwest and mid to upper level ridging\nremains across the central United States. An 850hPa thermal \nridge axis sets-up across the region Monday with temperatures at\nthis pressure level reaching 22-24C, translating to surface \nhighs in the upper 80s with little spread in the 25th-75th \npercentiles of the GEFS/EPS/GEPS and their AI counterparts. The \nwarmth will continue during the overnight hours when some near \nrecord warm low could be achieved, mainly across northeast Iowa.\n\nDespite the upper ridge remaining in place, a shortwave trough \nwill begin to flatten it on Monday, allowing additional \nshortwaves to traverse the mean flow through the middle of next \nweek. Periods of showers and storms will develop in response to \nthese upper level features with the potential for some stronger \nstorms, particularly on Monday when AI/ML guidance has broad low\nend probabilities for strong storm potential, though details \nare murky as far as when/where storms occur.",
